---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/monetisation/AdSlot.astro';
import { manifestEntry } from '../lib/seo/pages.ts';
const path = '/methodology/';
const entry = manifestEntry(path);
---

<BaseLayout
  title={entry?.title ?? ''}
  description={entry?.description ?? ''}
  path={path}
  indexable={entry?.indexable ?? false}
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'How these work', path },
  ]}
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>How these calculators work</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every figure on this site is produced by the same engine, from rules held as data. This page
      explains what that engine does, so you can check whether it matches your situation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="progressive">
    <h2 id="progressive">Progressive bands</h2>
    <p>
      Income tax in all five launch markets is progressive: a rate applies only to the slice of
      income that falls inside its band, not to your whole income. Someone who crosses into a 40%
      band does not suddenly pay 40% on everything — they pay 40% only on the part above the
      threshold.
    </p>
    <p>
      The engine applies each band to its own slice and shows the working, so you can see how much
      income was taxed at each rate rather than being asked to trust a single total.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="marginal">
    <h2 id="marginal">Marginal versus average rates</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>average rate</strong> is your total deductions divided by your total pay. The
      <strong>marginal rate</strong> is what comes off the next unit of pay you earn. They are almost
      never the same, and the marginal one is what determines how a pay rise feels.
    </p>
    <p>
      We measure the marginal rate rather than looking it up. The calculation is run again at a
      slightly higher salary and the difference in deductions is compared to the difference in pay.
      That matters because the true marginal rate often differs from the headline band rate — where
      an allowance is being withdrawn as income rises, for instance, each extra pound is taxed and
      also removes allowance that then gets taxed, producing an effective rate well above any
      published band.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="contributions">
    <h2 id="contributions">Contributions, levies and credits</h2>
    <p>
      Social contributions are not income tax and are shown on their own line. They typically have
      their own floor, their own bands, and often a ceiling above which no more is charged — which
      is why marginal rates can fall as income rises past a cap.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some systems reduce tax with an allowance deducted before the bands apply; others use credits
      subtracted from the tax due afterwards. The two are not interchangeable, and the engine models
      whichever the jurisdiction actually uses. Non-refundable credits are floored at zero — they
      can reduce tax to nothing but never produce a refund.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="annualisation">
    <h2 id="annualisation">Annualisation, and why results are estimates</h2>
    <p>
      Every result is calculated on a full tax year and then divided to show monthly, fortnightly,
      and weekly figures. Real payroll does not work that way: it withholds period by period, often
      cumulatively, and some contributions stop mid-year once an annual maximum is reached.
    </p>
    <p>
      So if you start a job part-way through the year, change salary mid-year, or receive very
      uneven pay, your actual deductions in any given month will differ from the figure shown here
      even when the annual total is right. That is why every result is labelled an estimate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot slot="guide-mid-content" />

  <section aria-labelledby="rounding">
    <h2 id="rounding">Rounding and precision</h2>
    <p>
      Money is never handled as a binary floating-point number anywhere in this codebase. Ordinary
      computer arithmetic cannot represent 0.1 exactly, and errors accumulate across the dozens of
      multiplications and divisions a tax calculation involves. Every monetary value here is a
      decimal from the moment it is entered until it is formatted for display.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rounding is applied at the stage the relevant tax authority specifies, and the policy for each
      jurisdiction is recorded in its ruleset alongside the rates. Where an authority rounds a
      figure down to the whole unit, we round down too, rather than doing what looks tidier.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="net-to-gross">
    <h2 id="net-to-gross">Working backwards from take-home pay</h2>
    <p>
      There is no formula that inverts a tax system with allowances, tapers and caps, so the
      net-to-gross calculator searches for the answer instead: it repeatedly narrows a range of
      possible gross salaries until it finds the one that produces your target.
    </p>
    <p>
      The answer is then checked by feeding it back through the ordinary forward calculation. If the
      round trip does not match, you are told so rather than shown a confident-looking figure. It is
      genuinely possible for no gross salary to produce a particular take-home figure, and when that
      happens the calculator says it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="unsupported">
    <h2 id="unsupported">What happens when we cannot answer</h2>
    <p>
      If a ruleset has not been verified against its official source, or you select an option the
      rules do not cover, the calculator does not estimate. It tells you what is missing and shows
      you the official source instead. A page in that state is also kept out of search results
      entirely, so it cannot be found by someone who has no way of knowing it is incomplete.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p><a href="/sources/">See the official sources behind each calculator →</a></p>
</BaseLayout>
